e warlord's men's screams — no, wait. Morgan Steele went back. Because fortune favors the bold, and because no one gets left, not even the bad guys. They dragged the warlord's men out by the collar.\n\nInside: wonders. the scholar Dr. Elena Vance, the scholar at heart, wept at the murals. Morgan Steele kept watch, because wonders in such places usually have teeth. They did: the floor was a trap, the walls were a trap, the air was a trap."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Price of Gold', 'text': "At the peak, they made their stand: outnumbered, outgunned, but holding the high ground and the moral ground, which as the scholar Dr. Elena Vance said weakly, 'is worth at least two guns.'\n\nthe warlord's men was delivered to the authorities with the evidence. a carved obelisk key was delivered to the museum, on loan, with Morgan Steele's name on the plaque and the scholar Dr. Elena Vance's name right beside it, where it belonged.\n\nthe warlord's men, bound and furious, offered everything: money, power, a share. Morgan Steele listened politely and said no politely, which infuriated the warlord's men more than any insult. Some people can't be bought. Some treasures can't be sold."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Real Treasure', 'text': "They stood before a carved obelisk key one last time. It had started all this: the legend, the race, the ruin, the rescue. Morgan Steele saluted it. Some doors, once opened, stay open forever.\n\nthe warlord's men got fifteen years and a memoir deal. Morgan Steele got the kid's wide eyes and the scholar Dr. Elena Vance's friendship and a new map already arriving by courier. No return address. Of course.\n\nfortune favors the bold — Morgan Steele wrote it in the expedition log, under the date, under the coordinates of the Amazon basin. Then closed the book. Then opened the new map."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Next Horizon', 'text': "Morgan Steele took one last look at the Amazon basin, green and gold and dangerous. It had given them a carved obelisk key, and taken nothing they weren't willing to give. Fair trade.\n\nThe plane — or boat, or truck, or camel — was already arranged. It always is, when a carved obelisk key calls. That's the thing about legends: they don't end. They just wait for the next believer.\n\nthe scholar Dr. Elena Vance was packed before Morgan Steele finished reading the note. 'Where to?' the scholar Dr. Elena Vance asked. Morgan Steele pointed at the map's edge, past the Amazon basin, past everything known. 'There,' Morgan Steele said."}]}

